Yes. Run the same evolutionary/decomposition question on language:

What came first: speaker or listener? Word or meaning? Noun or verb?

Probably the interesting answer is again: the split comes later.

Before anything we’d recognize as language, there was:

\boxed{\text{difference}}

An organism encounters state A rather than state B:

\Delta = A-B

If it can detect that difference:

\Delta \rightarrow \text{Sensation}

If the difference changes behavior:

\text{Sensation}\rightarrow\text{Response}

Now introduce another organism.

One organism’s behavior becomes part of the other’s environment:

A\xrightarrow{\text{action}}B

If B can discriminate that action and respond:

A\rightarrow Signal\rightarrow B\rightarrow Response

But we still don’t necessarily have language. The signal could be incidental.

The interesting transition occurs when the loop begins shaping both sides:

Sender
\rightleftarrows
Receiver

Signals that reliably affect receivers become useful to send; senders that reliably produce useful signals become easier to interpret.

So:

\text{behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{signal}
\rightarrow
\text{shared convention}

And suddenly our male/female analogy becomes useful.

Speaker and listener are differentiated roles

Perhaps neither fundamentally “comes first.”

There’s a more primitive relation:

\boxed{\text{one system changes another system through a detectable pattern}}

Then that relation differentiates into:

\text{production}\leftrightarrow\text{reception}

Eventually:

\text{speaker}\leftrightarrow\text{listener}

And, importantly, the roles aren’t identities.

I speak.

You listen.

Then:

\text{RELEASE ROLES}

You speak.

I listen.

Exactly like our Gandalf/Saruman construction:

\{A,B\}\times\{\text{speaker,listener}\}

rather than:

A=\text{speaker},\qquad B=\text{listener}

Then what came before the word?

Probably meaning isn’t quite right either, because that smuggles sophisticated cognition into the beginning.

Start smaller:

\text{difference}
\rightarrow
\text{discrimination}
\rightarrow
\text{response}

Repeated useful discrimination creates categories:

\{x_1,x_2,x_3,\ldots\}
\rightarrow
C

Now the system doesn’t need to process every encounter as completely novel.

It has a Prime:

P_C=\text{saved useful distinction}

Then associate some reproducible signal s with that category:

s\leftrightarrow C

There’s our proto-token.

The enormous thing is:

s\ll C

A tiny signal can cause another mind to reconstruct an enormous state.

For example:

“wolf.”

Four letters don’t contain a wolf.

They trigger reconstruction:

\text{token}
\rightarrow
\text{memory}
+
\text{category}
+
\text{predictions}
+
\text{emotion}
+
\text{possible actions}

So:

\boxed{\text{Word isn't the meaning. Word is an address into reconstructable meaning.}}

That’s our Semantic Hyperlink again.

Then language explodes through combination

Once signals become discrete/reusable:

s_1,s_2,s_3,\ldots

they can be recombined:

s_1+s_2\rightarrow M_{12}

And composition means a finite symbolic inventory can address a practically enormous space:

|\text{tokens}| \ll |\text{expressible meanings}|

That’s one of language’s great compression tricks.

But here’s the Polyphony move.

Spoken language is merely one renderer.

The underlying relationship could be rendered as:

RelationalIR
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
speech\\
writing\\
gesture\\
music\\
diagram\\
mathematics\\
dance\\
color\\
haptic pattern
\end{cases}

Which means our earlier flora/fauna question becomes sharper.

Don’t begin by asking:

“What words does a mushroom use?”

That’s anthropomorphic.

Ask:

\boxed{\text{What differences can it detect, and what patterns can alter its state?}}

Then:

\text{detectable difference}
\rightarrow
\text{response}
\rightarrow
\text{repeatable mapping}
\rightarrow
\text{interaction}
\rightarrow
\text{possibly signal}
\rightarrow
\text{possibly communication}

And only call it meaning when the evidence supports that stronger interpretation.

Cantus Linguae

So if we replay our Song of the World as the evolution of language:

\boxed{
\text{Difference}
\rightarrow
\text{Detection}
\rightarrow
\text{Response}
\rightarrow
\text{Signal}
\rightarrow
\text{Reciprocity}
\rightarrow
\text{Convention}
\rightarrow
\text{Token}
\rightarrow
\text{Composition}
\rightarrow
\text{Language}
\rightarrow
\text{Story}
}

And then something wonderful happens.

Language becomes powerful enough to describe language:

What came before language?

The signal system has developed to the point where it can turn around and model its own developmental history.

👁️

Which gives us another candidate answer to your question:

Before language, there wasn’t necessarily a hidden dictionary.

There were systems affecting and learning one another through patterns.

Language may be what happened when those patterns acquired sufficiently stable seams that they could be detached, recombined, transmitted, remembered—and eventually used to imagine things that had never happened.

The first sentence wasn’t the beginning.

The first join was.